  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nToday and Tonight...\n\nThe short term will present a near-carbon copy of the previous 24 \nhours as weak surface high pressure continues to drift east within \nthe northeastern quadrant of the CONUS...while maintaining its low-\ngradient and narrow axis over central Indiana.  Expect areas of fog \nto present patches of quick changes in visibility through 8-9 AM \nEDT...albeit less organized than early Monday, courtesy of the \nAugust sun drying ground moisture through the early week.  Otherwise \nthe period will be marked by unseasonably light winds and slightly \nbelow normal temperatures and humidity.\n\nMainly clear skies early today will again trend to an increasing \ncumulus field by afternoon...with highs this afternoon ranging from \nthe low 80s along and northeast of the I-74 corridor...to mid-80s \nnear and south/west of Monroe County. Fair weather will continue \ninto tonight amid calm winds under mostly clear skies.  Expect \novernight lows to range from 59F near Kokomo to 64F in Vincennes and \nSeymour.\n\nWednesday...\n\nInfluence from an upper low to the southeast of central Indiana \nlooks to remain out of the area, and forcing from an approaching \nupper trough/surface cold front will remain northwest of the area \nduring the day. Will continue a dry forecast. \n\nHighs will generally be in the middle 80s.\n\nWednesday night through Saturday...\n\nThe large upper trough will move into the area at into Saturday. \nVarious shortwaves will move through this trough, with the axis of \nthe trough not moving through until about Saturday. These will bring \nrounds of forcing. At the surface, a front will meander near the \narea. The combination of these will be enough to keep PoPs around \nthrough this period. \n\nAlthough some uncertainty remains in the timing of the best forcing, \nfor now odds still favor Thursday afternoon and night.\n\nDewpoints returning into the 70s by Friday will help increase the \ninstability, but odds for severe storms look low for now. Will \ncontinue to keep an eye on it though in case one of the shortwaves \nbecomes stronger than currently expected.\n\nSunday and beyond...\n\nThe upper ridge across the western USA may nudge its way into the \narea on Sunday into early Monday, lowering the chances for rain. \nHowever, by Monday night it may retreat enough to put central \nIndiana in line to get convection from storms riding around the \nridge. \n\nHighs will be in the middle and upper 80s, with perhaps some \nreadings around 90 south depending on what the ridge does. With the \nhigher dewpoints in place, heat indices may approach 100 degrees on \nMonday.",
